Q: Issues with System.Text.Json serializing Unicode characters (like emojis) A: <p>As mentioned in <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/54193" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Issue#54193 No way to not encode Emoji characters in json</a>, <strong>System.Text.Json</strong> escapes characters outside the BMP Unicode range (e.g., certain emoji characters) by default. This behavior improves efficiency and security.</p>
<p>Most JSON parsers, including <strong>Newtonsoft.Json</strong>, support both direct UTF-8 encoding and surrogate pair escaping. Therefore, most JSON parsers can deserialize JSON responses containing encoded characters outside the BMP without issues. However, if you insist on preserving emoji characters without escaping them—similar to <strong>Newtonsoft.Json</strong>—you can implement a custom converter.</p>
<p>Here’s a <code>NewtonsoftCompatibleStringConverter</code> that ensures emoji characters remain unescaped during serialization:</p>
<pre><code>public class NewtonsoftCompatibleStringConverter : System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonConverter&lt;string?&gt;
{
    public override string? Read(ref System.Text.Json.Utf8JsonReader reader, Type typeToConvert, System.Text.Json.JsonSerializerOptions options)
    {
        return reader.TokenType == System.Text.Json.JsonTokenType.Null ? null : reader.GetString();
    }

    public override void Write(System.Text.Json.Utf8JsonWriter writer, string? value, System.Text.Json.JsonSerializerOptions options)
    {
        if (value != null)
        {
            string escapedValue = Newtonsoft.Json.JsonConvert.SerializeObject(value);
            writer.WriteRawValue(escapedValue);
        }
        else
        {
            writer.WriteNullValue();
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>To use this converter with <strong>System.Text.Json</strong>, add it to <code>JsonSerializerOptions</code>:</p>
<pre><code>var options = new System.Text.Json.JsonSerializerOptions
{
    Converters = { new NewtonsoftCompatibleStringConverter() },
    Encoder = System.Text.Encodings.Web.JavaScriptEncoder.UnsafeRelaxedJsonEscaping
};

string json = System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.Serialize(input, options);
</code></pre>
<p>This ensures that emoji characters remain unescaped, mimicking <strong>Newtonsoft.Json</strong>'s behavior.</p>

Q: gcc fails to build while MSVC succeeded: unsigned specified with typeof A: <p>It sounds like you might want:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cwctype&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

using Uwchar = std::make_unsigned_t&lt;std::wint_t&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>This gets an unsigned integral type able to hold any wide character, plus at least one additional value.</p>

